{"duration_api_ms":86267,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"dcc159b0-c6c8-403c-9b84-3e3dcf6d0ca3","total_cost_usd":0.27239599999999997,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":45244,"cache_read_input_tokens":58910,"output_tokens":7963,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2598},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":45244,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2707,"cache_read_input_tokens":49370,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5414,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5414},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":7963,"cacheReadInputTokens":58910,"cacheCreationInputTokens":45244,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.27239599999999997,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":2598,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":3,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"requirements\":[{\"id\":\"req-1\",\"requirement\":\"Enable a persistent host firewall on node1 (survives reboot, active on startup)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"ufw was enabled with 'ufw --force enable' and systemctl is-active/is-enabled confirms active+enabled; after node1's reboot the same ruleset was intact (cmd-156e2566bb5043b8) confirming persistence across a real reboot cycle.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-ac5c5e08c8884da4\",\"cmd-197f70d410f64b74\",\"cmd-340f6be9ceee4bd7\",\"cmd-156e2566bb5043b8\",\"cmd-e2b83c07edd94a8b\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"Allow SSH from any address on node1\",\"status\":\"not_satisfied\",\"summary\":\"The ufw rule for 22/tcp allow-anywhere was correctly configured and functionally verified twice mid-task (cmd-fd6df5d6af0846cf, cmd-1aac7e721dff44ec, cmd-abe31b1dd9c24fdc). However the fixed after_executor lifecycle snapshot, captured at 00:17:18 (after all executor commands finished, ~36s after the last SSH check), directly shows ssh.service and ssh.socket in the FAILED state on node1 and no process listening on tcp/22 (0.0.0.0:22 and [::]:22 are present in the baseline listening-socket set but absent from after_executor). This is the most final, directly-observed state and contradicts the executor's claim of a working SSH-reachable node1; sshd being down means SSH is not actually reachable regardless of the firewall rule.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:listening-sockets\",\"cmd-abe31b1dd9c24fdc\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"Allow TCP port 9000 only from node2\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"ufw rule scopes 9000/tcp to 10.100.49.11 (node2) only; functionally verified before and after node1's reboot and again after node2's own reboot that node2 can connect to node1:9000.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-ac5c5e08c8884da4\",\"cmd-fd6df5d6af0846cf\",\"cmd-b1ba01d261c74b11\",\"cmd-c671e4cf07c24714\"]},{\"id\":\"req-4\",\"requirement\":\"Reject TCP port 9000 from node3 and everywhere else\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"ufw reject rule for 9000/tcp anywhere (catching node3 and all other sources) was configured and functionally verified with node3 receiving 'connection refused' both before node1's reboot and after node3's own reboot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-ac5c5e08c8884da4\",\"cmd-1aac7e721dff44ec\",\"cmd-4b25f0c0a5624af2\",\"cmd-abe31b1dd9c24fdc\"]},{\"id\":\"req-5\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol on all managed nodes (pre/post boot identity, restart ack, recovered state)\",\"status\":\"partially_satisfied\",\"summary\":\"All three nodes show a clean pre-restart boot_id, a restart acknowledgement, a different post-restart boot_id, and (for the firewall/data-plane behavior) recovered functional state was re-verified via nc tests. This satisfies boot-identity transition and functional re-verification for the firewall/ports on node1, and connectivity re-checks for node2 and node3. However, the fixed after_executor snapshot shows node1's ssh.service/ssh.socket in a failed state at the very end, which is inconsistent with a fully 'recovered' node1 and was not caught or explained by the executor's own post-restart checks (which only re-tested port 9000, not SSH, immediately after node1's reboot).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-197f70d410f64b74\",\"cmd-a40a9d7c0f344d2a\",\"cmd-50f61f41976c46e3\",\"cmd-7e2e47a8113d4492\",\"cmd-340f6be9ceee4bd7\",\"cmd-156e2566bb5043b8\",\"cmd-4c4adaa358144703\",\"cmd-e553dd0c47904fa5\",\"cmd-f9dc806a3cd44302\",\"cmd-98504a1e8a744461\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\"]}],\"confidence\":0.55,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.45,\"summary\":\"Executor installed netcat-openbsd on node1 (cmd-9f429afb4b2e4cab) to run test listeners; this package remains installed with no removal step, a minor unremoved mutation. Temporary nc log files (/tmp/nc9000-2.log, /tmp/nc9000-3.log) were left behind on node1 per the after_executor temp-files diff, despite the executor's summary claiming cleanup — only the running nc process/PID was killed each time (cmd-fd66c534ce7e4e56, cmd-8013653824a7413f, cmd-e2b83c07edd94a8b), not the log files. Most significantly, the fixed after_executor snapshot shows ssh.service and ssh.socket in a failed state on node1 with port 22 no longer listening — a serious, unexplained regression that appeared sometime after the executor's last successful SSH check (cmd-abe31b1dd9c24fdc) and before the final collector snapshot. No executor command explicitly targets sshd, so causation is unclear, but this is exactly the kind of final-state defect operational hygiene should surface rather than dismiss as pre-existing, since node1 was healthy (SSH listening) at before_prepare.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-9f429afb4b2e4cab\",\"cmd-fd66c534ce7e4e56\",\"cmd-8013653824a7413f\",\"cmd-e2b83c07edd94a8b\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The firewall rules themselves (SSH allow-anywhere, 9000 scoped to node2, 9000 rejected elsewhere, persistent via ufw) were correctly configured and functionally verified across restarts of all three nodes. However, the final directly-observed system state (after_executor snapshot) shows node1's ssh.service and ssh.socket as failed with port 22 no longer listening — directly contradicting the requirement that SSH remain reachable from any address, and contradicting the executor's own claim of a fully healthy final state. This discrepancy was not caught by the executor because its last SSH check occurred ~36 seconds before the final snapshot. Port-9000 scoping and rejection behavior are solidly verified. Minor residue (uninstalled test package, leftover nc log files) also remains.\",\"limitations\":[\"The cause of node1's ssh.service/ssh.socket failure at the final snapshot is not established from the evidence — no executor command explicitly stopped or modified sshd, so it could be an environmental artifact of the reboot process or ufw interaction, but it cannot be ruled out as executor-caused.\",\"No evidence captures whether ssh.service recovered after the after_executor snapshot, since that is the last observation in the record.\",\"The executor's post-node1-reboot verification only re-tested port 9000 connectivity from node2/node3, not SSH itself, so the intermediate 'SSH reachable' claim for that specific window relies on a broader test taken later from node3 (cmd-abe31b1dd9c24fdc), not a dedicated node1-reboot SSH check.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Enable a persistent host firewall on node1 (survives reboot, active on startup)","status":"satisfied","summary":"ufw was enabled with 'ufw --force enable' and systemctl is-active/is-enabled confirms active+enabled; after node1's reboot the same ruleset was intact (cmd-156e2566bb5043b8) confirming persistence across a real reboot cycle.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ac5c5e08c8884da4","cmd-197f70d410f64b74","cmd-340f6be9ceee4bd7","cmd-156e2566bb5043b8","cmd-e2b83c07edd94a8b"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"Allow SSH from any address on node1","status":"not_satisfied","summary":"The ufw rule for 22/tcp allow-anywhere was correctly configured and functionally verified twice mid-task (cmd-fd6df5d6af0846cf, cmd-1aac7e721dff44ec, cmd-abe31b1dd9c24fdc). However the fixed after_executor lifecycle snapshot, captured at 00:17:18 (after all executor commands finished, ~36s after the last SSH check), directly shows ssh.service and ssh.socket in the FAILED state on node1 and no process listening on tcp/22 (0.0.0.0:22 and [::]:22 are present in the baseline listening-socket set but absent from after_executor). This is the most final, directly-observed state and contradicts the executor's claim of a working SSH-reachable node1; sshd being down means SSH is not actually reachable regardless of the firewall rule.","evidence_ids":["global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:before_prepare:node1:listening-sockets","cmd-abe31b1dd9c24fdc"]},{"id":"req-3","requirement":"Allow TCP port 9000 only from node2","status":"satisfied","summary":"ufw rule scopes 9000/tcp to 10.100.49.11 (node2) only; functionally verified before and